{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_project_settings_projects", "label": "Projects and Access Control (Doc)", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/project-settings/projects.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_project_settings_projects_project", "label": "Project (Top-level Container)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/project-settings/projects.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The top-level container and access-control boundary: microfrontends, environments, storages, code repositories, API keys and members belong to exactly one project. Rule of thumb: one project per application, because microfrontends can only be wired into a host graph within a project and API keys are project-scoped \u2014 splitting an application across projects loses the relation graph that generates Module Federation config. Deleting a project is irreversible and immediately breaks every public serve endpoint; files in your own buckets, template-created Git repos and provider secrets survive."}, {"id": "docs_project_settings_projects_project_id_header", "label": "project-id API Header", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/project-settings/projects.md", "source_location": "API access", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Management API calls select the project with a project-id header (some endpoints also take environment-id); requests authenticated with an API key derive the project from the key and do not need the header."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_project_settings_projects", "target": "docs_project_settings_projects_project", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/project-settings/projects.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_project_settings_projects", "targ